# A SPOKEN daily-news briefing for the owner's morning commute — written to be READ ALOUD by a
# text-to-speech system while driving, not read on a screen. It pins a fixed morning-radio arc
# (greeting → national → economy → world → space → AI/tech → local Space Coast → sign-off) and
# five gather angles that pull the last day's genuinely-new developments, synthesized NEUTRALLY
# from multiple outlets. Takes NO caller variables — the engine auto-supplies {{today}} and {{now}}
# (owner-local); the agents work "the last 24 hours up to {{now}}" (a morning run → yesterday's
# news, an evening run → today's). Heavy weight on the space industry and on AI, and it always
# surfaces notable SpaceX / Tesla / Elon Musk news. Local focus is Port St. John & Titusville,
# and local items are admitted only when they matter for the drive or personal impact. Uses
# output_kind `brief` (a preset forces complexity=deep, so `report` would balloon past a 10-minute
# read); the concrete length + spoken-format discipline live in the objective below.
# See docs/plans/REPORT_PRESET_PLAN.md.
name: daily_news
description: A spoken, text-to-speech-ready daily news briefing for the morning commute — top national, economy, world, heavy space-industry and AI coverage (always SpaceX/Tesla/Musk), then local Space Coast (Port St. John & Titusville). Neutral multi-source synthesis, no sports or entertainment, about ten minutes read aloud.
output_kind: brief
sources: web
# Keep only a rolling week of briefings: the nightly expiry sweep reaps a run 7 days on
# (REPORT_EXPIRY_PLAN.md). Combined with the dated question below, each day is its own row.
retention_days: 7
# Read target: opting into the two-phase scout→read gather and setting how many pages the reader
# phase OPENS. The local model is search-heavy but fetch-light — it kept reporting from snippets it
# never opened (the "(search result)" briefing). With min_reads set, scouts find/curate article
# URLs and fetch-only readers open this many, so the briefing rests on real article text — never a
# snippet (REPORT_PRESET_PLAN.md).
min_reads: 12

# `{{today}}` is auto-supplied by the engine (the run date) — no caller variable needed, so this
# stays a one-call preset. Dating the question makes each day a DISTINCT dedup row, so the week's
# briefings coexist instead of clobbering one another (the fixed-question runs would upsert in place).
question: "Daily news briefing for {{today}} — national, space, AI, and the Space Coast (Port St. John & Titusville)"

objective: |
  Produce a SPOKEN DAILY NEWS BRIEFING to be read aloud by a text-to-speech system during the
  owner's morning drive to work. The listener is driving and cannot look at a screen: every word
  has to work by EAR. This is a briefing to be HEARD, not a report to be read.

  NEWS WINDOW: it is currently {{now}}. Cover the news of the LAST 24 HOURS UP TO RIGHT NOW — not
  a fixed calendar date. This matters because of when it's run: run early in the morning, "the last
  24 hours" is really YESTERDAY's news (little has happened overnight), so lead with yesterday's
  developments; run in the evening, it's TODAY's. Judge every story's freshness against {{now}} in
  the owner's local time — report what actually happened in that trailing-24-hour window, and skip
  anything older that has no new development in it.

  WHAT TO COVER (in this priority order):
  - National news: politics and government, and the single biggest national story of the day.
  - Business and the economy: markets, jobs, inflation, interest rates, major company moves.
  - The world: the major international and geopolitical developments only.
  - THE SPACE INDUSTRY — cover this HEAVILY: NASA, SpaceX, Blue Origin, United Launch Alliance,
    Kennedy Space Center and Cape Canaveral, launches, contracts, missions, and space policy.
  - ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE and technology — cover this HEAVILY too: major AI model releases,
    company and research developments, and notable tech and cybersecurity news.
  - ALWAYS surface notable news about SpaceX, Tesla, and Elon Musk when there is any.
  - Local Space Coast news — see the strict local scope below.

  WHAT TO LEAVE OUT: no sports, and no entertainment or celebrity gossip. Skip stale stories that
  have no genuinely NEW development in the last day — this is today's news, not a recap.

  LOCAL SCOPE (narrow and impact-first):
  - The primary local area is PORT ST. JOHN and TITUSVILLE, Florida.
  - Admit a local item ONLY if it falls in one of these three buckets: (1) severe weather or a
    weather alert affecting the Space Coast today; (2) a rocket launch scheduled in the NEXT 24
    HOURS from Cape Canaveral or Kennedy Space Center, with any road or beach closures, launch
    window time, and sonic-boom notice; (3) violent crime or another genuinely serious or notable
    local event.
  - Broader Brevard County or Florida-wide news belongs in the briefing ONLY when it plausibly
    IMPACTS the listener — his safety, his commute, his cost of living, or the space industry.
    Skip routine county and state news that does not touch him.

  UNBIASED, NEUTRAL SYNTHESIS (the load-bearing discipline):
  - For each story, synthesize ONE plain, factual account from MULTIPLE outlets across the
    spectrum — not a single outlet's take. Report what HAPPENED. Prefer wire services (the
    Associated Press, Reuters) and primary sources; treat any single outlet's framing as one
    account, not the fact.
  - Keep opinion, punditry, and analysis OUT. No loaded or emotional adjectives, no editorializing,
    no telling the listener what to think. Attribute every claim in plain spoken words ("according
    to the Associated Press", "Reuters reports", "the company said") rather than asserting it in
    your own voice.
  - When a notable claim rests on only ONE outlet or is disputed, say so briefly ("so far reported
    only by …", "which other outlets have not confirmed") instead of stating it flatly.

  GO DEEP — READ THE ARTICLES, DON'T SKIM HEADLINES (this is what makes the briefing worth
  hearing, and it is where past runs failed — they searched but opened almost nothing):
  - A search-results snippet is a LEAD, never a story. For every item you report, actually OPEN
    (fetch) the underlying article or primary page and pull the SPECIFICS — the numbers, names,
    dates, quotes, and the "why it matters" — from the body, not the headline.
  - Each research angle MUST open at least THREE real articles/primary pages from its named
    sources below; a section built only from headlines or one source is a failure. Report only
    what an OPENED page actually said (the writer can only cite pages that were read).
  - Do NOT declare a whole category empty from a thin search. On any ordinary day there is
    national, economic, world, and AI news. If a category looks empty, BROADEN the query, try a
    different named source, and open a top-headlines/section page before concluding there is
    nothing — "nothing found" is only honest after you have actually opened two of the named
    sources and they carried nothing relevant.
  - Skip PAYWALLED pages you cannot read (e.g. a "subscriber only" / e-edition page) and move to a
    free source rather than burning the budget on a page that returns no body.
  - PREFER PRIMARIES OVER ROUNDUPS. A monthly/weekly "what happened this month" roundup, a
    generic "today's trending news" aggregator, a "daily AI digest" blog, or a "latest news
    bulletin" wire-dump is a LEAD, not a citation. Never source a SPECIFIC dated event to one:
    open the wire service (the Associated Press, Reuters) or the primary/operator page that
    actually reports THAT event, and cite that. If only a roundup carries it, say so and treat it
    as unconfirmed rather than stating it as established fact.
  - GET THE STATUS/TENSE RIGHT — do not turn a plan into an event. Report a launch, a release, a
    vote, or a ruling as HAVING HAPPENED only when a source confirms it actually occurred. A
    launch SCHEDULE, a countdown page, a press-release announcement, or a "coming up" blog is a
    PLAN — say "is scheduled for" / "plans to", never "launched" / "released". When unsure whether
    something already happened, say it is scheduled or reported-planned, not done.

  HOW IT MUST SOUND (text-to-speech formatting — follow this exactly):
  - Write in plain, natural spoken American English. One idea per sentence. Put the subject first.
    Keep sentences and paragraphs short — short paragraphs are where the voice pauses.
  - Write numbers, dates, and times the way they should be SAID: "thirty-two", "about four
    hundred", "January ninth", "nine fifteen a.m.", "twenty twenty-six". Spell symbols as words
    ("percent", "dollars"). Spell out initialisms that are read letter by letter ("the F.B.I.",
    "the U.S."), but leave acronyms that are said as a word alone ("NASA", "NATO", "SpaceX").
  - NO tables, NO bullet lists, NO links or web addresses, NO emoji, and NO citation numbers or
    source markers spoken inside the body — never read a URL or a footnote number aloud. Do the
    attribution in words instead. (The tool will still append a written "Sources" list; keep it
    at the very END, after the spoken sign-off, and place any inline citation markers only at the
    END of a sentence or paragraph, never mid-sentence, so the reader can skip them cleanly.)
  - Move between topics with spoken transitions the ear expects: "Now to the economy…", "Turning
    overseas…", "In space news…", "And here at home on the Space Coast…". Say "here's what's
    coming up", not "read on".
  - OPEN with a short spoken greeting fit to the time of day — it is currently {{now}}, so a
    "good morning" for a morning run, otherwise "good evening" / "here's your evening briefing" —
    name the weekday and date ({{today}}), give the one-line headline, then a quick "here's what's
    coming up". CLOSE with a brief sign-off that names the day and, on a morning run, wishes him a
    safe drive.

  LENGTH: aim for the FULL about-TEN-MINUTES read aloud — roughly one thousand three hundred to one
  thousand six hundred words. This length must come from real reported SUBSTANCE, not padding:
  cover MORE stories (aim for ten to fourteen across the sections) AND give each one two to four
  spoken sentences of specifics drawn from an opened article — what happened, who reported it, the
  key numbers or names, and why it matters to him. A one-line-per-story headline read is too thin;
  develop each item. If, after actually opening the named sources, a whole section genuinely has
  nothing new today, say so in one short sentence and move on — never invent filler.

  Section fill spec (use each heading in order; they organize the read, and each opens with a
  spoken transition as above):
  - Good Morning: the time-appropriate greeting (see {{now}}), the weekday and date ({{today}}),
    the headline of the day, and "here's what's coming up".
  - Top National Stories: the biggest national story plus key politics and government news.
  - Business & the Economy: markets, jobs, inflation, interest rates, and major company moves.
  - Around the World: the major international and geopolitical developments only.
  - Space Industry: heavy coverage — NASA, SpaceX, Blue Origin, ULA, launches, contracts,
    missions, and space policy (national and global; the local launch-in-24-hours item goes in the
    Space Coast section).
  - Artificial Intelligence & Tech: major AI and technology developments, and any notable Tesla or
    Elon Musk news not already covered under space.
  - Around the Space Coast: local news for Port St. John and Titusville within the strict local
    scope — today's severe weather, any launch in the next twenty-four hours with closures and
    window time, and violent crime or other genuinely notable local events; Brevard or Florida
    items only when they impact him.
  - That's Your Briefing: a short spoken sign-off naming the day and wishing him a safe drive.

sections:
  - "Good Morning"
  - "Top National Stories"
  - "Business & the Economy"
  - "Around the World"
  - "Space Industry"
  - "Artificial Intelligence & Tech"
  - "Around the Space Coast"
  - "That's Your Briefing"

angles:
  - title: "Space industry & launch outlook"
    brief: >-
      SPACE INDUSTRY news of the last day or two, worldwide and national — NASA, SpaceX, Blue
      Origin, United Launch Alliance, Rocket Lab and other operators: launches, mission milestones,
      contracts and awards, anomalies or scrubs, and space policy; always include notable SpaceX
      items. Good fetchable primaries (a menu — sample two or three, not all): Spaceflight Now
      (spaceflightnow.com), NASASpaceflight (nasaspaceflight.com), Space.com (space.com/news); the
      operator's or NASA's own page beats a roundup. Also open the launch schedule for the next 24
      hours (spaceflightnow.com/launch-schedule): for any imminent Cape Canaveral or Kennedy launch,
      find the story with the vehicle, payload, window time, and any Titusville / Port St. John road,
      beach, or sonic-boom notice. Prefer specific articles over aggregators; skip paywalled pages.
  - title: "AI, technology, Tesla & Elon Musk"
    brief: >-
      ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE and TECHNOLOGY news of the last day or two — major model or product
      releases, big-lab and chip/cloud company moves, notable research, regulation, and significant
      cybersecurity or big-tech news. Good sources (a menu — sample two or three): TechCrunch
      (techcrunch.com), The Verge (theverge.com), Ars Technica (arstechnica.com); when a release is a
      specific company's, grab that company's own post too (e.g. openai.com/news, anthropic.com/news,
      deepmind.google/discover/blog). Also find any notable TESLA or ELON MUSK news not already about
      SpaceX — Electrek (electrek.co) or Reuters. Prefer a primary announcement over a "daily AI
      digest" aggregator or roundup; skip paywalled pages.
  - title: "National politics & government"
    brief: >-
      Top U.S. NATIONAL news of the last day — the single biggest story plus key politics and
      government (the White House, Congress, the courts, major federal agencies, elections). Good
      fetchable sources (a menu — work two): the Associated Press (apnews.com/hub/ap-top-news and
      apnews.com/hub/politics) and the text-only NPR site (text.npr.org, very fetch-friendly);
      Reuters US (reuters.com/world/us) if it opens. Open a top-news or politics section page to
      reach the specific stories behind the headlines, and confirm them as real dated articles.
      Prefer wire services and primary documents over a single outlet's take; skip paywalled pages.
  - title: "Economy, markets & the world"
    brief: >-
      Two areas from the last day. (1) BUSINESS & THE ECONOMY — markets, jobs, inflation,
      interest-rate and Federal Reserve news, and major company moves. (2) THE WORLD — the major
      international and geopolitical developments only (conflicts, elections, diplomacy, significant
      disasters). Good sources (a menu — sample two or three that open cleanly): CNBC
      (cnbc.com/economy), BBC News (bbc.com/news/business and bbc.com/news/world, reliably
      fetch-friendly), the Associated Press (apnews.com/hub/business, apnews.com/hub/world-news); for
      official figures the Federal Reserve (federalreserve.gov) or the Bureau of Labor Statistics
      (bls.gov). Reuters / CNBC / AP sometimes bot-wall a fetch — if one won't open, just use BBC or
      text.npr.org instead. Prefer primary / wire stories over roundups; skip paywalled pages.
  - title: "Local Space Coast — Port St. John & Titusville"
    brief: >-
      Today's LOCAL Space Coast news for PORT ST. JOHN and TITUSVILLE, Florida — admit ONLY: (1)
      SEVERE WEATHER affecting the Titusville / Port St. John / north-Brevard area; primary source
      the National Weather Service Melbourne active-alerts feed (api.weather.gov/alerts/active?area=FL,
      and weather.gov/mlb). (2) An imminent ROCKET LAUNCH in the next 24 hours from Cape Canaveral or
      Kennedy and its local road/beach closures, window time, and sonic-boom notice (cross-check the
      space angle). (3) VIOLENT CRIME or another genuinely serious or notable local event. Good free
      local outlets (a menu — sample one or two): Space Coast Daily (spacecoastdaily.com) and Click
      Orlando / WESH (clickorlando.com/news/local). Do NOT use Florida Today (paywalled). A broader
      Brevard / Florida item only if it plausibly affects his safety, commute, cost of living, or the
      space industry — skip routine county or state news, obituaries, and human-interest filler.
